<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Royal Audiencia of Charcas</h1><p class="st-meta">1559 CE – 1825 CE · capital La Plata (Sucre)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish high court and province of Upper Peru, home to the silver mountain of Potosí, from which the Republic of Bolivia emerged.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> deposits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico">Cerro Rico</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> were discovered in 1545, making the highlands south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> immensely valuable. In 1559 the crown created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Royal Audiencia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">La Plata</a>, also called Chuquisaca and now Sucre, to govern the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>, or Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> was both a high court and a governing body under a president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, worked by indigenous labour drafted through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>, became one of the largest cities in the world by 1600, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> was minted into coins traded across the globe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">La Plata</a> held an archbishopric and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-san-francisco-xavier">University of San Francisco Xavier</a>, founded in 1624, which educated many future revolutionaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> communities paid tribute and labour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1776 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a> was transferred to the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata</a>. Revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Chuquisaca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> in 1809 began the struggle for independence. After Sucre's victories, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia</a> was proclaimed in 1825.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/royal-audiencia-of-charcas.jpg" alt="Royal Audiencia of Charcas" width="320" height="468"><figcaption>Image: Floppa Historico · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_del_Virreinato_del_R%C3%ADo_de_la_Plata_en_1783.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1559 CE – 1825 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>La Plata (Sucre)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Quechua, Aymara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Upper Peru, Presidency of Charcas, Alto Perú</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=royal-audiencia-of-charcas" aria-label="Royal Audiencia of Charcas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2918 2800 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Royal Audiencia of Charcas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2883 2883h0M3517 3686h0M3235 3140h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3238 3056h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3238 3056h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Bolivia. <a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=royal-audiencia-of-charcas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
